1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
The label on this white vinegar says five

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:03,360
per cent acidity.

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:05,920
How could you check how much ethanoic acid

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:06,960
is really in it?

5
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:09,360
A pH meter won't do it well.

6
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:12,160
Ethanoic acid is weak, so most of it

7
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:15,120
stays as whole molecules, and the meter only

8
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:17,280
senses the few that have ionised.

9
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,240
You could work back through K a, but

10
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:22,160
a reading of two point four could mean

11
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:24,880
anything from about zero point seven to one

12
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:26,480
point one moles per litre.

13
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:29,360
You need a method that counts every acid

14
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:30,000
molecule.

15
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:31,840
That's a titration.

16
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,000
So how does it work, and how do

17
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:36,000
its numbers give a concentration?

18
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,600
The idea is to react the unknown with

19
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:43,120
a solution whose concentration you know exactly, a

20
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:46,320
standard solution, and measure precisely how much it

21
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:47,120
takes.

22
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:49,640
A pipette delivers an exact volume of the

23
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:53,200
vinegar, called an aliquot, into a conical flask,

24
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:54,800
with a few drops of indicator.

25
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:58,880
A burette adds sodium hydroxide, the titrant, until

26
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:00,880
the acid has just been used up.

27
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:03,360
The volume delivered is the titre.

28
00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:06,726
The point where the amounts match the equation

29
00:01:06,807 --> 00:01:09,126
exactly is the equivalence point.

30
00:01:09,687 --> 00:01:11,846
You see it as the endpoint, when a

31
00:01:11,927 --> 00:01:15,366
well-chosen indicator changes colour very close to it,

32
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:17,926
here colourless to faint pink.

33
00:01:18,247 --> 00:01:20,646
And as in the weak acid heat video,

34
00:01:20,687 --> 00:01:22,886
the weak acid gets fully counted.

35
00:01:23,127 --> 00:01:26,566
Hydroxide removes hydronium and takes protons from whole

36
00:01:26,647 --> 00:01:30,326
molecules, so the equilibrium keeps shifting right until

37
00:01:30,407 --> 00:01:32,486
every molecule has reacted.

38
00:01:32,567 --> 00:01:34,646
So the titre depends on how much acid

39
00:01:34,727 --> 00:01:36,966
there is, not how strong it is.

40
00:01:38,167 --> 00:01:41,126
Everything rests on that known concentration.

41
00:01:41,607 --> 00:01:43,606
The most reliable way to get one is

42
00:01:43,687 --> 00:01:46,486
to weigh out a primary standard: a solid

43
00:01:46,567 --> 00:01:49,526
that's very pure, stable in air, and doesn't

44
00:01:49,647 --> 00:01:51,446
take up water as you weigh it, so

45
00:01:51,527 --> 00:01:53,766
its mass gives its moles exactly.

46
00:01:54,487 --> 00:01:57,526
Anhydrous sodium carbonate works if it's dried in

47
00:01:57,607 --> 00:01:58,566
an oven first.

48
00:01:59,207 --> 00:02:01,766
Sodium hydroxide fails that test.

49
00:02:02,007 --> 00:02:04,166
It soaks up water and reacts with carbon

50
00:02:04,247 --> 00:02:06,886
dioxide from the air, so its solution is

51
00:02:06,967 --> 00:02:11,126
first standardised: titrated against a primary standard, to

52
00:02:11,247 --> 00:02:12,966
find its real concentration.

53
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:16,966
Back to the vinegar: ten point zero zero

54
00:02:17,127 --> 00:02:20,886
millilitres, titrated with zero point five zero zero

55
00:02:21,047 --> 00:02:23,846
zero moles per litre sodium hydroxide.

56
00:02:24,167 --> 00:02:26,806
A rough first run just locates the endpoint,

57
00:02:26,887 --> 00:02:28,486
so it isn't used.

58
00:02:28,567 --> 00:02:31,606
The careful titres were eighteen point six zero,

59
00:02:31,687 --> 00:02:35,206
eighteen point five five, eighteen point five five,

60
00:02:35,327 --> 00:02:38,406
and eighteen point nine zero millilitres.

61
00:02:38,487 --> 00:02:41,126
Titres are usually called concordant when they agree

62
00:02:41,287 --> 00:02:44,486
within zero point one zero millilitres, so the

63
00:02:44,607 --> 00:02:47,206
eighteen point nine zero is left out.

64
00:02:47,287 --> 00:02:49,286
The average of the other three is eighteen

65
00:02:49,447 --> 00:02:50,646
point five seven.

66
00:02:51,867 --> 00:02:53,226
Now four steps.

67
00:02:53,467 --> 00:02:57,386
First, the balanced equation: ethanoic acid and sodium

68
00:02:57,467 --> 00:02:59,906
hydroxide react one to one.

69
00:03:00,267 --> 00:03:03,146
Second, moles of the solution you know everything

70
00:03:03,227 --> 00:03:06,346
about: n equals c V, with the volume

71
00:03:06,427 --> 00:03:07,346
in litres.

72
00:03:07,627 --> 00:03:10,506
Zero point five zero zero zero times zero

73
00:03:10,667 --> 00:03:13,386
point zero one eight five seven is nine

74
00:03:13,547 --> 00:03:15,626
point two eight five times ten to the

75
00:03:15,827 --> 00:03:17,946
minus three moles of hydroxide.

76
00:03:19,167 --> 00:03:21,086
Third, the mole ratio.

77
00:03:21,647 --> 00:03:24,446
One to one, so there's also nine point

78
00:03:24,527 --> 00:03:26,446
two eight five times ten to the minus

79
00:03:26,607 --> 00:03:29,366
three moles of ethanoic acid in the flask.

80
00:03:29,647 --> 00:03:33,406
Fourth, divide by the aliquot's volume, zero point

81
00:03:33,487 --> 00:03:36,446
zero one zero zero zero litres.

82
00:03:36,487 --> 00:03:39,166
The concentration is zero point nine two eight

83
00:03:39,287 --> 00:03:42,606
five moles per litre, four significant figures, like

84
00:03:42,647 --> 00:03:43,366
the data.

85
00:03:43,647 --> 00:03:45,966
That's about five point six grams of acid

86
00:03:46,047 --> 00:03:48,126
in every hundred millilitres.

87
00:03:48,167 --> 00:03:50,766
The label's five per cent means five grams,

88
00:03:50,887 --> 00:03:51,886
so it's a bit more.

89
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:55,780
The ratio isn't always one to one.

90
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:59,660
To standardise hydrochloric acid, a student pipettes twenty

91
00:03:59,724 --> 00:04:03,180
five point zero zero millilitres of zero point

92
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:06,380
zero five zero zero zero moles per litre

93
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:10,220
sodium carbonate, a primary standard, into the flask,

94
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:12,460
and runs the acid in from the burette.

95
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:15,660
The concordant titres average twenty two point four

96
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:17,500
five millilitres.

97
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:20,780
Sodium carbonate takes two hydrochloric acid for every

98
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:24,620
one of itself, because carbonate accepts two protons.

99
00:04:25,834 --> 00:04:29,673
Moles of carbonate: zero point zero five zero

100
00:04:29,834 --> 00:04:33,033
zero zero times zero point zero two five

101
00:04:33,194 --> 00:04:36,233
zero zero, one point two five zero times

102
00:04:36,314 --> 00:04:37,993
ten to the minus three.

103
00:04:38,154 --> 00:04:40,713
Times two for the ratio: two point five

104
00:04:40,954 --> 00:04:43,193
zero zero times ten to the minus three

105
00:04:43,434 --> 00:04:45,273
moles of hydrochloric acid.

106
00:04:45,514 --> 00:04:48,073
That acid came from the burette, so divide

107
00:04:48,154 --> 00:04:50,793
by the titre, zero point zero two two

108
00:04:50,914 --> 00:04:53,993
four five litres: zero point one one one

109
00:04:54,114 --> 00:04:55,673
four moles per litre.

110
00:04:55,914 --> 00:04:58,233
It doesn't matter which solution is where.

111
00:04:58,354 --> 00:05:00,593
You always go from the known, through the

112
00:05:00,634 --> 00:05:02,393
ratio, to the unknown.

113
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:04,960
So here's what to remember.

114
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:08,640
Known to moles, ratio, unknown.

115
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:11,200
Start with n equals c V for the

116
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:14,080
solution you know everything about, use the balanced

117
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:18,240
equation's ratio, then divide by the unknown's volume.

118
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:20,960
And average only concordant titres.

119
00:05:22,167 --> 00:05:22,886
Your turn.

120
00:05:23,367 --> 00:05:26,486
A twenty five point zero zero millilitre aliquot

121
00:05:26,567 --> 00:05:29,766
of sulfuric acid was titrated with zero point

122
00:05:29,927 --> 00:05:33,046
one zero zero zero moles per litre sodium

123
00:05:33,127 --> 00:05:33,926
hydroxide.

124
00:05:34,487 --> 00:05:37,366
The titres were twenty three point four five,

125
00:05:37,487 --> 00:05:40,326
twenty three point seven zero, twenty three point

126
00:05:40,447 --> 00:05:43,046
four zero, and twenty three point five zero

127
00:05:43,207 --> 00:05:44,486
millilitres.

128
00:05:44,767 --> 00:05:47,766
Calculate the concentration of the sulfuric acid.

129
00:05:47,927 --> 00:05:49,126
Pause here to answer.

130
00:05:59,534 --> 00:06:02,893
Twenty three point seven zero isn't concordant, so

131
00:06:02,974 --> 00:06:06,013
the average is twenty three point four five.

132
00:06:06,094 --> 00:06:08,493
That's two point three four five times ten

133
00:06:08,654 --> 00:06:11,053
to the minus three moles of hydroxide.

134
00:06:11,694 --> 00:06:14,733
Sulfuric acid gives two protons, so the ratio

135
00:06:14,814 --> 00:06:16,653
